### Why is throwing an exception expensive?

The cost comes from three steps that happen every time you create and throw an exception.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class ExceptionCostBreakdown {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Cost breakdown of: throw new RuntimeException(msg)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step 1: fillInStackTrace()");
        System.out.println("  Native call walks entire thread stack");
        System.out.println("  Creates StackTraceElement per frame");
        System.out.println("  Cost: 1-5 microseconds");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step 2: Stack unwinding");
        System.out.println("  JVM pops frames, checks exception table");
        System.out.println("  Repeats until handler found");
        System.out.println("  Cost: 0.5-2 microseconds");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step 3: Object allocation");
        System.out.println("  Exception object + StackTraceElement[]");
        System.out.println("  Cost: minimal but adds to GC pressure");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Total: 2-7 microseconds per throw");
        System.out.println("Normal return: ~0.01 microseconds (200-700x cheaper)");
    }
}
```

```text
Cost breakdown of: throw new RuntimeException(msg)

Step 1: fillInStackTrace()
  Native call walks entire thread stack
  Creates StackTraceElement per frame
  Cost: 1-5 microseconds

Step 2: Stack unwinding
  JVM pops frames, checks exception table
  Repeats until handler found
  Cost: 0.5-2 microseconds

Step 3: Object allocation
  Exception object + StackTraceElement[]
  Cost: minimal but adds to GC pressure

Total: 2-7 microseconds per throw
Normal return: ~0.01 microseconds (200-700x cheaper)
```

### How do stackless exceptions reduce cost?

Override fillInStackTrace() to skip the stack walk. The exception has no stack trace but is roughly 100 times faster to create.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class StacklessOptimizationDemo {

    static class NormalException extends RuntimeException {
        NormalException(String msg) { super(msg); }
    }

    static class StacklessException extends RuntimeException {
        static final StacklessException INSTANCE = new StacklessException("validation");
        StacklessException(String msg) { super(msg); }
        @Override public synchronized Throwable fillInStackTrace() { return this; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 100_000;

        long start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            try { throw new NormalException("test"); } catch (NormalException e) { }
        }
        long normalMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;

        start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            try { throw StacklessException.INSTANCE; } catch (StacklessException e) { }
        }
        long stacklessMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;

        System.out.println("Normal:    " + normalMs + "ms (" + n + " throws)");
        System.out.println("Stackless: " + stacklessMs + "ms (" + n + " throws)");
        System.out.println("Speedup:   ~" + (normalMs / Math.max(stacklessMs, 1)) + "x");
    }
}
```

```text
Normal:    185ms (100000 throws)
Stackless: 4ms (100000 throws)
Speedup:   ~46x
```

### When should you use Optional instead of exceptions?

For expected conditions (key not found, empty result, invalid format), Optional is 100 to 300 times cheaper than throwing.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;

public class OptionalVsExceptionDemo {

    static Map<String, BigDecimal> accounts = Map.of("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000"));

    // SLOW: exception for expected "not found"
    static BigDecimal slowFind(String id) {
        BigDecimal balance = accounts.get(id);
        if (balance == null) throw new RuntimeException("Not found: " + id);
        return balance;
    }

    // FAST: Optional for expected "not found"
    static Optional<BigDecimal> fastFind(String id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(accounts.get(id));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Exception approach: expensive for missing keys
        try {
            slowFind("UNKNOWN");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Exception: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // Optional approach: cheap, no exception
        BigDecimal balance = fastFind("UNKNOWN").orElse(BigDecimal.ZERO);
        System.out.println("Optional: Rs." + balance);

        // Real-world pattern:
        // tradeRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException(id))
        // Only throws for truly missing trades, not for expected "not found"
    }
}
```

```text
Exception: Not found: UNKNOWN
Optional: Rs.0
```

### What is the summary of optimization techniques?

| Technique | Cost per throw | When to use |
|---|---|---|
| Normal exception | 2-7 microseconds | Rare, unexpected errors |
| Stackless exception | 0.05 microseconds | Hot path, known throw point |
| Pre-built singleton | 0.02 microseconds | Validation, control flow |
| Optional/return code | 0.01 microseconds | Expected "not found" |
| No exception (if/else) | 0.001 microseconds | Normal control flow |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **-XX:-OmitStackTraceInFastThrow.** The JVM may omit stack traces for frequently thrown exceptions. Add this flag to force full traces for debugging.
- **Netty and high-performance frameworks** use stackless exceptions for channel pipeline exceptions.
- **JMH benchmarks.** Use JMH (Java Microbenchmark Harness) for accurate exception cost measurements. The numbers above are approximate.
- **try-catch does NOT prevent JIT optimization.** Only thrown exceptions cause deoptimization. The try block itself is optimized normally.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the most expensive part of throwing? (fillInStackTrace native stack walk.)
- Is try-catch expensive? (No. The try body has zero cost. Only throwing is expensive.)
- When should you use exceptions vs Optional? (Exceptions for exceptional situations. Optional for expected "not found.")
- How do you create a stackless exception? (Override fillInStackTrace() to return this.)
